text:
customer-ops: Hey team — multiple customers reporting streaming responses that cut off mid-output after ~20-40s. Clients see a partial event and then connection closed. Repro on our side with a slow reader shows server aborting write with EAGAIN/EPIPE around the same timeframe.

lucy: Can you paste a snippet of server logs and a sample request_id?

customer-ops: request_id=rs_20260312_7f3a2c. Server log: `sse: write failed: write tcp 10.0.0.5:443->10.0.2.9:52344: write: connection reset by peer` then immediately `stream aborted: backpressure_timeout`. Also noticed metric "stream_send_queue_bytes" climbing to ~16MB then a hard abort.

support-bot: Logged incident 4092, pinging eng-runtime.

jordan: Is this behind the corp proxy / nginx? We recently enabled gzip and left proxy_buffering defaults. Nginx tends to buffer chunked responses which interacts badly with slow consumers.

customer-ops: Yes — traffic goes through our ELB -> nginx-ingress (proxy_buffering on) -> app.

mike: If the proxy buffers, the server sees writes succeed locally (data goes to proxy) but the proxy stalls sending to client, filling kernel buffers until we hit TCP backpressure and writes block/timeout. That matches EAGAIN then EPIPE once client gives up.

lucy: Any client info? Language and runtime?

customer-ops: Node client using eventsource polyfill (eventsource npm) on mobile devices with flaky networks. We also tested with `curl --limit-rate 10` to simulate slow reader and reproduced the partial output.

backend-oncall: Can you try disabling nginx proxy_buffering on the route and add `X-Accel-Buffering: no` from the app? Also ensure `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` with no Content-Length. Sample nginx config:

```
location /v1/streams/ {
  proxy_pass http://redwood-app;
  proxy_buffering off;
  proxy_request_buffering off;
  proxy_http_version 1.1;
  proxy_set_header Connection "";
  proxy_set_header Host $host;
}
```

jordan: Also set `proxy_send_timeout` and `send_timeout` defaults higher. But real fix is to flush after each SSE chunk and avoid gzip for SSE (`gzip off` or `Cache-Control: no-transform`).

customer-ops: Did the team change anything recently? We didn't touch app code. nginx was upgraded last week and defaulted to proxy_buffering=on.

mike: That explains it. Buffering + slow consumer = server-side queue growth then aborts. For immediate mitigation: set `proxy_buffering off` and add header `X-Accel-Buffering: no` on stream responses. Also recommend `Content-Encoding` absent (no gzip) so proxy doesn't buffer.

lucy: For debugging, please capture: `ss -tnp | grep 52344` around the incident, `netstat -anp` and server logs with request_id. Also dump our internal metric `stream_send_queue_bytes{route="/v1/streams"}` and `socket_write_blocked_total`.

customer-ops: Attaching a minimal repro curl command we used:

```
# server side: start a simple SSE endpoint
# client side simulate slow reader
curl -N http://staging.redwood/v1/streams?session=test --limit-rate 5
```

support-bot: FYI billing note: partial responses will still count toward token usage. If we need to credit affected requests, please list request_ids and customer emails.

billing: Please DM the request_ids and we'll evaluate credits case-by-case.

backend-oncall: Longer-term change: runtime should detect sustained write-blocked state and gracefully degrade (trim queue / send disconnect frame) rather than hard abort. I'm opening an eng-runtime ticket with repro steps.

mike: For a deterministic repro script, use this Node snippet to emulate a slow reader:

```
const http = require('http');
http.get('http://staging.redwood/v1/streams?test', (res) => {
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    process.stdout.write(chunk);
    // pause read to simulate slow client
    res.pause();
    setTimeout(() => res.resume(), 15000);
  });
});
```

customer-ops: We'll try disabling buffering and retest. If it still aborts we'll send full tcpdump and pcap.

ingress-proxy: Note: some managed proxies (cloud LB or CDN) will re-chunk and buffer. If you route through a CDN, ensure origin response has `Cache-Control: no-transform` and SSE-friendly headers.

lucy: Action items: 1) customer-ops flip proxy_buffering off on nginx for streaming routes (done?), 2) customer-ops run the curl/node repro and attach logs + request_ids if issue persists, 3) eng-runtime to investigate graceful degradation and add telemetry, 4) billing to review credits if customers impacted.

customer-ops: proxy_buffering off applied to /v1/streams now, retesting. Initial test: full stream flows to curl with `--limit-rate 5` and no immediate abort. Will monitor production and report. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:

support-bot: Closing interim incident 4092 (monitoring). Reopen if errors reappear.
